/*
# Form

The place a set of fields is submitted from. The controls inside it — every `TextInput`
and `ValueControl` that exists and is not disabled — **join it by being inside it**; there
is nothing to register. It holds no values: they live wherever the program keeps them
(the App, a model class, a dataset). It knows what is wrong, says so on submit, sends, and
carries a service's refusals back to the fields they name.

```declare
App [ width = 360, height = 240, fill = white,
    email: string = "",
    signIn: Form [ x = 20, y = 20, width = 300,
        layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 12 ],
        Field [ label = "Email", stacked = true,
            TextInput [ width = 300, name = "email", purpose = email, required = "Enter your email address.",
                text = { app.email }, onInput(v: string) { app.email = v },
                validate(v: string) -> string { return v.includes("@") ? "" : "Enter an email address like name@example.com." } ] ],
        Button [ label = "Sign in", primary = true, onClick() { parent.submit() } ],
        send() { app.email = "" }
        ]
    ]
```

**The normal case sends to a service.** Give the form its `source` — the `DataSource`
that sends — and `submit()` judges every member, sends when nothing is wrong, keeps
`pending` while the request is out, and reads a refusal's body back onto the members by
`name`. A message that names no member becomes the form's `error`. The source's own
`onLoad` is where a success goes on, as ever.

**A form with no service** leaves `source` out and says what sending is: `send()` runs
when `submit()` finds nothing wrong, and the program saves however it likes there — to a
`Dataset`, a file, the host page (the example above). An outside check (a name already
taken in local data) is said with `refuse`. Slow work there sets `pending` itself while it
runs.

**Membership** is the form's subtree, with three edges:
- an overlay (a `Sheet`, `Dialog`, `Popover`) is a boundary: a control in it belongs to a
  `Form` inside the overlay, not to one around the place it is declared. A control set
  apart on purpose names its form: `form = { app.payment }`;
- a control inside another control is part of it: a `Combobox`'s entry field belongs to
  the combobox, which is the member;
- a member that is not shown (it, or a view above it, has `visible = false`) or is
  disabled does not count: it is not judged, and its problems hold nothing up.

## submit()
Judges every member now, so every problem shows at once, each under its own field —
fields the person never reached and fields autofill filled without focusing included.
Withdraws the last refusals. If anything is wrong, `showProblems()`; otherwise `send()`.
Does nothing while `pending`. Return in
a single-line `TextInput` inside the form calls it (or moves on, under `enterMoves`), unless
the field declares an `onEnter` of its own.

## enterMoves
Return in a single-line field takes the person **to the next field** — in reading order,
among the fields that count — and submits from the last; an on-screen keyboard's Return
key says "Next" on the way and "Go" on the last. Off (the default), Return submits from
any field, as on the web. A field that declares its own `onEnter` decides for itself.

## next()
`next(from)` takes the focus from a field to the next one that counts, in reading order,
or submits the form when `from` is the last. What Return does under `enterMoves`; a field's
own `onEnter` may call it.

## showProblems()
What submit does when something is wrong. By default: when more than one field has a
problem, it announces how many; it takes the focus to the first (in reading order),
whose own message a screen reader then hears; a form-level `error` is announced. Override
it to focus a summary of your own, scroll, or stay silent.

## send()
What `submit()` does when nothing is wrong. By default it sends `source` (and does nothing
when there is none). A form with no service overrides it to save its own way; one with a
`source` that also wants to do something first calls `super.send()` after. A send of the
program's own has succeeded when it refused nothing — once `pending` is false, if it set
it — and the fields are then judged afresh, so a field emptied for the next entry says
nothing.

## source
The `DataSource` the form sends — a write: `method = "POST"`, its `body` built from the
program's values. `pending` follows the request the form sent; when the service refuses,
its `errorBody` goes through `refusal` onto the members. Leave it null to send another way
(`send()`).

## pending
True while the request the form sent is out; `submit()` does nothing meanwhile. A form
with no `source` sets it itself around slow work in `send()`, and back to false when the
work is done. Keep the Submit button
focusable while pending (say so in its label); `submit()` already ignores the press.

## submitted
Whether `submit()` has been tried since the last success. A presentation that shows
nothing until a submit reads it.

## problems
Every counted member's current `problem`, in reading order: `{ name, message, control }`
each. It does not wait for judging, so it is the fact a summary, a banner or a count is
built on.

## valid
Whether nothing is wrong: no member has a problem and `validate()` is satisfied.

## error
The message that belongs to no field: a refusal that names none (an account locked), a name
no counted member has, the form's own `validate()` on submit, or `failedMessage` when the
request failed without a readable reply. Shown wherever the program places it.

## validate()
The form's own rule, for what belongs to no one field — return `""` when it is fine, or
the sentence. A rule that does belong to a field goes on that field (`validate` there may
read any other value).

## refuse()
`refuse(problems)` lays problems on the form from anywhere: each `{ name, message }` lands
on the counted member of that `name` as its `refused`, and the rest become `error`. A
refusal reaches it from `source` by itself; a service that answers success with errors in
the body (GraphQL does) calls it from the source's `onLoad`.

## refusal()
`refusal(body)` reads a refusal's body into problems. It understands one field
(`{ "field", "message" }`), a list (`{ "errors": [ … ] }` with `field`, `path`, a JSON
`pointer` or JSON:API's `source.pointer`, and `message`, `detail` or `title`), and a bare
`{ "message" }`, which names no field. A pointer is read as a dotted name
(`#/address/city` is `address.city`) and matched exactly. Override it to map
a service's own shape, its codes to the program's sentences, or another language.

## refusedWith()
`refusedWith(body)` is the program's own reaction when the service refuses, given the body:
undo a reservation the refusal names, empty a field the service will not take again. The
order is fixed: the refusal is laid on the members first (each named field's `refused`, the
rest in `error`), then `refusedWith` runs, then `showProblems()` — so what it changes is
what the person is then shown.

## failedMessage
What `error` says when the request failed with nothing readable — no reply at all, or a
reply that is not a refusal. Say it in the program's own words.

## members
The controls in the form, counted or not. Kept by the controls themselves.

## _form
**Internal.** what the controls inside read to find their form.

## _sending
**Internal.** the form's own request is out.

## _out
**Internal.** whether `source` is loading — watched to learn how the form's request ended.

## _join()
**Internal.** A control inside joins the form.

## _leave()
**Internal.** A control that goes leaves it.

## _counts()
**Internal.** Whether a member counts: enabled, and shown up to the form.

## _collect()
**Internal.** Gathers the counted members' problems in reading order.

## _ordered()
**Internal.** The counted members in reading order: top to bottom, then left to right.

## _enter()
**Internal.** What Return in a member does: `next` under `enterMoves`, else `submit`.

## _enterHint()
**Internal.** What a member's on-screen Return key says: "next", or "go" on the last.

## _proceed()
**Internal.** Submit's second half, once the judging has settled.

## _landed()
**Internal.** Decides whether a send of the program's own went well.

## _succeeded()
**Internal.** A send went well: the fields are judged afresh.

## _nameOf()
**Internal.** Reads a pointer or path as a dotted name.
*/

// One thing wrong: the member's address, what to say, and the member, when there is one.

schema FormProblem [ name: string, message: string, control?: any ]

class Form [
    // what the controls inside read to find their form

    _form: any = { this },

    source: DataSource? = null,
    failedMessage: string = "That didn't go through, and nothing was saved. Try again.",

    members: any[] = [],
    _join(c: any) { if (!members.includes(c)) members = [...members, c] },
    _leave(c: any) { members = members.filter((m) => m !== c) },

    // a member counts while it is enabled and shown, up to the form

    _counts(c: any) -> boolean {
        if (c.disabled === true) return false
        let v = c
        while (v != null && v !== this) {
            if (v.visible === false) return false
            v = v.parent
            }
        return v === this || c.form === this
        },

    problems: FormProblem[] = { this._collect() },
    _collect() -> FormProblem[] {
        const out: FormProblem[] = []
        for (const c of this._ordered()) if (c.problem != "") out.push({ name: c.name, message: c.problem, control: c })
        return out
        },

    // the counted members in reading order: top to bottom, then left to right

    _ordered() -> any[] {
        return members.filter((c) => this._counts(c)).sort((a, b) => {
            const p = a.rootOrigin()
            const q = b.rootOrigin()
            return p.y != q.y ? p.y - q.y : p.x - q.x
            })
        },

    // Return in a field: submit, or — with enterMoves — on to the next field, and
    // submit from the last

    enterMoves: boolean = false,
    next(from: any) {
        const ms = this._ordered()
        const at = ms.indexOf(from)
        const to = at < 0 ? null : ms[at + 1] ?? null
        if (to == null) { submit(); return }
        if (typeof to.focusField == "function") to.focusField()
        else Focus.focus(to)
        },
    _enter(from: any) { if (enterMoves) next(from); else submit() },
    _enterHint(from: any) -> string {
        if (!enterMoves) return "go"
        const ms = this._ordered()
        return ms.indexOf(from) == ms.length - 1 ? "go" : "next"
        },
    validate() -> string { return "" },
    valid: boolean = { this.problems.length == 0 && this.validate() == "" },

    error: string = "",
    submitted: boolean = false,
    _sending: boolean = false,
    pending: boolean = { this._sending },

    submit() {
        if (pending) return
        submitted = true
        error = ""
        for (const c of members) {
            if (c.refused != "") c.refuse("")
            if (this._counts(c)) c.judged = true
            }
        afterSettle(() => this._proceed())
        },
    _proceed() {
        const own = validate()
        if (problems.length > 0 || own != "") {
            if (own != "") error = own
            showProblems()
            return
            }
        send()
        if (source == null) afterSettle(() => this._landed())
        },

    // a send of the program's own has gone well when, once nothing is pending,
    // it refused nothing: the fields are judged afresh from here

    _landed() {
        if (pending || error != "" || members.some((c) => c.refused != "")) return
        _succeeded()
        },
    _succeeded() {
        for (const c of members) c.judged = false
        submitted = false
        error = ""
        },
    send() {
        if (source == null) return
        _sending = true
        ;(source as any).fetch()
        },

    showProblems() {
        if (error != "") Accessibility.announce(error, "assertive")
        const ps = problems
        if (ps.length == 0) return
        if (ps.length > 1) Accessibility.announce(ps.length + " fields need attention.", "assertive")
        const c = ps[0].control
        if (typeof c.focusField == "function") c.focusField()
        else Focus.focus(c)
        },

    refusedWith(body: any) { },

    refuse(list: FormProblem[]) {
        const rest: string[] = []
        for (const p of list) {
            const c = p.name == "" ? null : members.find((m) => m.name == p.name && this._counts(m))
            if (c != null && p.message != "") c.refuse(p.message)
            else if (p.message != "") rest.push(p.message)
            }
        error = rest.join(" ")
        },

    refusal(body: any) -> FormProblem[] {
        const say = (e: any) => e == null ? "" : String(e.message ?? e.detail ?? e.title ?? "")
        const out: FormProblem[] = []
        if (body != null && typeof body == "object") {
            if (Array.isArray(body.errors)) {
                for (const e of body.errors) {
                    const where = e.field ?? e.path ?? e.pointer ?? e.source?.pointer ?? e.source?.parameter ?? ""
                    if (say(e) != "") out.push({ name: this._nameOf(String(where)), message: say(e) })
                    }
                }
            else if (body.field != null && say(body) != "") out.push({ name: this._nameOf(String(body.field)), message: say(body) })
            if (out.length == 0 && say(body) != "") out.push({ name: "", message: say(body) })
            }
        if (out.length == 0) out.push({ name: "", message: failedMessage })
        return out
        },
    _nameOf(where: string) -> string {
        let p = where.startsWith("#") ? where.slice(1) : where
        if (p.startsWith("/")) p = p.slice(1)
        if (p.startsWith("data/attributes/")) p = p.slice(16)
        return p.split("/").join(".")
        },

    // what became of the request the form sent

    _out: boolean = { this.source != null && this.source.loading },
    trackChanges = ["_out", "pending"],
    onChange(e: ChangeEvent) {
        if (source == null) {
            if (submitted && !pending && e.changed.some((c) => c.name == "pending")) _landed()
            return
            }
        if (_out || !_sending) return
        _sending = false
        if ((source as any).failed) {
            const body = (source as any).errorBody ?? null
            refuse(refusal(body))
            refusedWith(body)
            afterSettle(() => this.showProblems())
            }
        else _succeeded()
        }
    ]
